#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Download the data files research asks for into research/data/, and record where each came from.

Research lists the files it wants in research/data/wanted.txt, one https address per line, but it
can't download them: its web tool reads pages without saving files, and it can't run commands. So
you run this, in this folder:

  ./fetch-data                      download what wanted.txt lists that isn't here yet, and any
                                    recorded file that's missing, as in a new clone. It lists them
                                    and asks first; --yes doesn't ask
  ./fetch-data add FILE [ADDRESS]   record a file you downloaded yourself, copying it into
                                    research/data/. Its record says "by hand": nothing checked
                                    that it came from ADDRESS
  ./fetch-data refresh NAME ...     download recorded files again, keeping the new version of any
                                    that the publisher has changed
  ./fetch-data self-check           test the sandbox and the refusals, for check.py

Each file is saved exactly as the website served it, and research/data/fetched.tsv records its
address, when it was fetched, its size and its SHA-256. Then convert-docs makes text copies of the
spreadsheets, archives, compressed files and documents, in research/data-copies/. The build
container reads research/data/ at /data and the copies at /data-copies, both read-only. "Data for
the build agent" in the README has more.

Research reads the web, so a misleading page could choose the addresses. Each download runs in
bubblewrap, which sees system folders but not your home folder or this project, and writes only to
an empty folder of its own. Only https addresses are fetched, and only from public internet
addresses, checked again at every redirect, so nothing on this machine or your local network can
be copied in for research to read. Whoever downloaded a file, these are refused: files over 4 GB,
a web page in place of a data file, formats that run code when they're loaded, and archives that
would unpack outside their folder, or that hold links, files Claude Code loads as instructions or
a git repository.

This script lives outside research/ and build/ on purpose: nothing the host runs should live where
an agent can write.
"""
